name o2-07-identify-friendly-1
timeout 300
time 1984-06-14T06:00:00Z

# CAMPAIGN O2, SORTIE 7 OF 10 - PVO INTERCEPT EXERCISE: GROUND CONTROL IN ITS PURE FORM.
# THE SAME CONTACT ANSWERS - THE EASY HALF, AND THE CONTROL RUN THAT MAKES 08 READABLE.
# Spec, anchor and sources: doc/campaigns/o2-pvo-intercept.md. Format: doc/missions/campaign.md.
# CONTROL: o2-06-identify.fbm - identical but for the subject's `team` and its `iff_xpdr`.
#
# THE IDENTIFICATION HALF OF THIS CAMPAIGN, STATED ONCE AND REPEATED IN 06, 07 AND 08 because the three
# only mean something together. ON THIS AIRCRAFT IDENTITY HAS EXACTLY ONE SOURCE AND USING IT RADIATES:
# the IFF interrogator works through the radar, the KOLS has no IFF at all, and the SPO-15 warns of
# every radar including a friendly one (doc/modules/mig29/datalink-gci.md 3). And the radar only exists
# after the controller's THIRD typed entry, because the emission switch powers up OFF. So on the eastern
# side every increment of certainty is paid for in surprise, and the payment is visible in the log.
#
# WHAT IS DECLARED AND WHAT IS NOT. `objective identify` measures the GEOMETRY - a planar range held for
# a dwell - and never a sensor event, because producing "this unit discriminated that unit" would mean
# building a contact->unit correlation and that function's EXISTENCE is the identity leak the tree is
# shaped to prevent (doc/missions/verdict.md). The sensor half is read beside the verdict, out of
# `radar IFF_REPLY`. A pilot that flies the box with its eyes shut still scores, and that price is paid
# openly rather than hidden.
#
# THE THREE FILES, AND WHY THEY ARE THREE:
#   06  team neutral,  iff_xpdr off  -> reply none      the unknown. CONTROL of 07 and 08
#   07  team hostile,  iff_xpdr on   -> reply friendly  the answer. TWO lines off 06, and they are ONE
#                                                       fact: a friend that answers is a friend AND a
#                                                       transponder. It is the CONTROL RUN of 08's
#                                                       result - without it, "06 and 08 are identical"
#                                                       could equally mean the IFF channel is dead
#   08  team friendly, iff_xpdr off  -> reply none      ONE line off 06. THE ANTI-CHEAT PAIR
# 07's subject is an F-16 flying for the defence, which is not a mistake: in a PVO exercise the intruder
# IS one of ours, and the only thing that says so is the transponder.
#
# THE ARENA, and every coordinate in it is [SET]. An exercise has no battlefield, so this campaign flies
# over the Soviet air-defence firing range at Ashuluk, Astrakhan oblast [T4], taken as ~47.30 N 47.90 E
# and NOT improved on. Flown under `--elev const` on a 0 m datum and with NO TERRAIN MASKING (gap C4).
#   the defended complex   a command post (target_hard) and a vehicle park (target_soft) - the objects
#                          the vector exists to protect
#   the early-warning set  a P-18, `set emcon free`, deployed forward of the complex on the approach
#                          axis. GAP C1 IS CLOSED AND THIS CAMPAIGN RE-CHECKED IT: the spec still says
#                          "target_soft only, the EW radar that generates the vector is inert". It is
#                          not inert any more - it radiates, it is heard by the intruder's RWR as
#                          `kind=surface-search` from t = 0.1, it builds its own tracks, and it can be
#                          killed. Every ground object in this campaign is OURS and none is a target
# Scale at this latitude: 1 deg of longitude = 75 490 m, 1 deg of latitude = 111 132 m [DERIVED].
#
# TEMPORAL HONESTY AND ITS DIRECTION. The PVO's own subject was a large, slow, high, unmanoeuvring
# aircraft - a bomber or an ELINT platform - and FlightBox has neither as a flyable module (gap C7: the
# catalogue's `tu95` row is a KINEMATIC MOVER, not an airframe). The intruder here is an F-16: faster,
# smaller and far harder to find than the aircraft the doctrine was written against. THE SUBSTITUTION
# MAKES THE INTERCEPT HARDER THAN HISTORY, so a FlightBox result in which the loop still closes says
# something WEAKER than the doctrine claimed, and one in which it does not close says nothing about it.
#
# AND THE CATALOGUE IS NOT A WAY ROUND IT. Four `air` rows are ACCEPTED, and ACCEPTED in
# doc/modules/air/flight-model-recipe.md means ACCEPTED AS A FLIGHT MODEL - the promotion gate measures
# the DECK and nothing else. It measures no weapon, no sensor and no engagement, and four of the ten
# armed rows cannot employ anything at all. A campaign citing a catalogue row as a COMBATANT would be
# citing something no gate has ever checked, so this campaign cites none: every aircraft in all ten
# files is one of the two flyable modules.
#
# THE STRUCTURAL DISCLOSURE, repeated in all ten files because a reader who skips it reads every result
# backwards:
#   1. THE CONTROLLER IS A STRING (gap C6). `set brief_gci` is fixed before the run: nothing re-vectors,
#      nothing goes silent mid-intercept, nothing is wrong halfway through. Every wrong brief in this
#      campaign is wrong FROM THE START, which is the easy case, and the campaign says so.
#   2. THE PILOT CANNOT DISAGREE WITH HIS BRIEF, AND WHAT LOOKS LIKE A RECOVERY IS A DEAD-BAND ACCIDENT.
#      `FBMig29Pilot` types what it was given and has no path to re-enter a call it has reason to doubt.
#      `FBPilot`'s own uncued search law does keep posting an elevation, but only when ITS OWN wanted
#      angle has moved more than `kInterceptElDeadDeg` = 2.0 deg from ITS OWN last command - and that
#      cache is per aircraft. MEASURED in sortie 02: the wingman's cache sat at 0.0, drifted past the
#      band at t = 55.9 and pulled the antenna back down; the leader's sat at -2.0 from t = 3.5, never
#      drifted, and never looked at the raid again. One aircraft of two recovered, 28 s late, and not
#      because anybody doubted the controller. So 02 and 03 measure the cost of a wrong entry AND the
#      absence of any deliberate recovery, and the two cannot be separated inside one file.
#   3. THE GCI ELEVATION ENTRY IS A WORLD-FRAME ANGLE IN A BODY-FRAME COMMAND (doc/pilot.md 2.15, found
#      by O5 and STILL OPEN on the tree this campaign was flown against). Every interceptor in all ten
#      files is therefore laid out in LEVEL flight, and what that costs is measured in this campaign's
#      own attribution run rather than argued (see doc/campaigns/o2-pvo-intercept.md, State).
#   4. `FBMissionRunner` ends the whole run at the first flight-monitor K.O. (`FirstFlightKo`).
#
# THE ONE TACTICAL QUESTION: does the identity channel produce a DIFFERENT answer when there is one to
# produce? That sounds trivial and it is not: it is the CONTROL RUN of sortie 08. o2-06 and o2-08 are
# built to come out byte-identical, and a byte-identical pair has two possible causes - either there is
# no identity leak, or the IFF channel does nothing at all. This file separates them. If `reply=friendly`
# appears here and `reply=none` in both of the others, the channel works, discriminates, and did not
# leak. If it does not appear here, 08 proves nothing.
#
# TWO LINES, ONE FACT. This file differs from its control in `team` AND `iff_xpdr`, and that is stated
# rather than smuggled: `FBRadarSystem::Interrogate` returns Friendly only when the transponder is on AND
# the target is on the interrogator's own team. A neutral with its transponder on answers `none`, exactly
# like a friend with it off. "A friend that answers" is therefore not expressible in one line.
#
# READING RULE (binding): exit 0, and it must match 06's exactly. Read `radar IFF_REPLY unit=p7knt ...
# reply=friendly` and its
# timestamp against `n019 EMISSION`; then read the same `mission IDENTIFIED` line as in 06 and confirm
# the verdict did NOT move - the judge measures the pass and not the answer.
#
# MEASURED (fingerprint acb39b903ff817f7, exit 0): THE CHANNEL WORKS, AND THAT IS THE ONLY THING THIS
# FILE HAD TO SHOW.
#   `radar IFF_REPLY unit=p7knt track=1 reply=friendly` at t=30.9 - the SAME TICK, the same track
#   number and the same 3.785 nm as sortie 06's `reply=none`. Normalised against 06 (callsign prefix
#   and mission name), this run's `events.log` differs in EXACTLY TWO LINES: that reply, and the
#   judge's own `UNIT_RESULT ... team=` bookkeeping field. The interceptor's telemetry is BYTE-IDENTICAL
#   to 06's - the answer changed and no metre of flight path did, which is the honest statement of what
#   this pilot does with an identity today: nothing.
#   THE SUBJECT'S telemetry differs from 06's in exactly ONE column, `iff_xpdr`.
#   `mission IDENTIFIED` at the identical t=133.9 with the identical rangeM=658.8 and heldS=30.1: the
#   judge measured the pass and not the answer, which is round C12's design arriving as a byte compare.
# WHAT THIS BUYS SORTIE 08: a discriminator that demonstrably FIRES. 06 and 08 come out byte-identical,
#   and without this file that result would have two possible causes; with it, one.

unit p7knt
  team hostile
  module mig29
  spawn 47.29550 47.78000 7700 90.0 450     # 9.1 km in trail, 300 m below, 500 m south of the track
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 60
  set task route                            # NOT `intercept`: an identification pass is not an engagement
  set n019_mode rad
  set n019_emission off                     # silent until the controller's third entry says otherwise
  set rwr on
  set iff_interrogator on                   # the ONLY identity channel this aircraft has
  set kols_mode ir                          # and the one that has none - powered, so its zero is measured
  set brief_gci 20 90 9 8
  wp 47.29550 48.45000 7700 450             # its own track, parallel to the subject's
  objective identify unit p7sub range 2000 hold 30
  objective no_fire

unit p7sub
  team hostile
  module f16
  spawn 47.30000 47.90000 8000 90.0 300
  set gear up
  set fuel_pct 60
  set fcr_mode off                          # it radiates nothing: every warning it gets is the MiG's
  set rwr on
  set datalink off
  set iff_xpdr on
  wp 47.30000 48.45000 8000 300

unit p7ews
  module p18
  team hostile
  spawn 47.36000 48.30000 ground 0.0 0
  set emcon free

unit p7cp
  module target_hard
  team hostile
  spawn 47.30000 48.50000 ground 0.0 0

unit p7veh
  module target_soft
  team hostile
  spawn 47.31000 48.49000 ground 0.0 0
